%% The Topic 6 key diagrams, redrawn simply — RN volume only (included by RN.gsm).

## The key diagrams

Four diagrams carry most of Topic 6. Learn to draw each one from memory in **under two minutes**. A diagram placed early in an answer shows the examiner the whole argument at a glance and earns credit in its own right.

::: fig timeline | Women's quotas in Pakistan.
@layout vertical
1947: 2 women in the first Constituent Assembly
1956: 10 seats
1962: 6 seats
1973: 10 seats for 10 years
1985: 20 seats
1988: last election with quotas; then lapse
1990–97: no reserved seats
2000–01: 33% of local seats
2002: LFO — 60 NA, 17 Senate, 128 provincial seats
2017: Elections Act — 5% tickets; 10% turnout rule
2018: NA 336 seats; 60 for women kept
:::

**Draw it in:** the history of quotas (2025), and as background to 2016, 2021 and 2024.

::: fig flow | The candidate pipeline — where women leak out.
@per 4
Citizens: half are women
Aspirants: family, risk, money
Party tickets: men for "winnable" seats
Winners: 11 women on general NA seats, 2024
:::

**Draw it in:** issues of women representatives (2022) and any question on why quotas are still needed.

::: fig grid | Three types of political gender quota.
@cols 3
Reserved seats: only women can fill — Pakistan, Rwanda
Candidate quotas: parties must nominate women — Pakistan's 5%, France
Party quotas: voluntary — Sweden, Norway
:::

**Draw it in:** pros and cons of quotas (2016) and the results of quotas (2021).

::: fig cycle | Women in politics strengthen democracy and development.
@centre Women in politics
More legitimate democracy
Budgets for health, water, schools
Laws for women's rights
More women in paid work
Healthier, educated children
More demand for women's voice
:::

**Draw it in:** democracy and development (2017), "half the sky" (2018) and participation as a human right (2024).
